    %main.sub-main
      .sub-content
        %h1 Tutorials
        %p.sub-lead Getting PteronautOS into the air — from source to first flap, step by step.

        %h2 01 · Build the firmware
        %p Clone the repository and build the receiver target with PlatformIO.
        %pre
          %code pio run -e PteronautOS_ESP8285_2400_RX

        %h2 02 · Flash the receiver
        %p Wire an FTDI adapter to the UART pads, hold BOOT, and write the firmware image. On the ESP8285 the auto-reset line is unreliable, so keep BOOT held while powering the board.
        %pre
          %code esptool.py --port /dev/cu.usbserial-XXXX --baud 115200 --before no_reset write_flash --flash_mode dout --flash_size 1MB --flash_freq 40m 0x0 firmware.bin

        %h2 03 · Bind your transmitter
        %p Flash the same binding phrase on transmitter and receiver. The UID is derived from the phrase — same phrase on both ends, they find each other. The receiver status in the web UI shows the UID.

        %h2 04 · Wire the servos
        %p Three servo channels: left wing, right wing, and crest rudder. The MPU6050 gyro hangs on the I2C bus.
        %p See the %a{href: "../hardware/"} Hardware page for pin maps and wiring.

        %h2 05 · First flap
        %p Power up, centre the trims, and advance the throttle. At zero ferocity the wings should beat in a clean sine. If a servo slams to an endstop, check the flapping centre offset and the servo pulse limits.

        %h2 06 · Tune via the Web UI
        %p Join the receiver's WiFi network and open the config panel in a browser. Adjust ferocity, glide angle, flapping centre offset, and rudder yaw/roll mixing — no reflash needed.
        %p The full reference lives in the %a{href: "../documentation/"} Documentation page.
